The vocabulary for narrowing REGISTER_ONE.

EXPERIMENT-zzzza implements the usual arithmetic conversions, which means both
operands of a binary operator have to be reduced to the chosen type before the
operator runs. emit_narrowing_cast already did that for REGISTER_ZERO;
REGISTER_ONE is the LEFT operand and neither table could touch it.

Which side is signed is not fixed -- `got != 0x87654321` and `0x87654321 !=
got` are both legal C and reach different registers -- so guessing one is not
an option.

Only the four-byte unsigned entry is added, because that is the only
conversion zzzza emits. Adding the other five by symmetry would put five
encodings in the tables that nothing has assembled, which is the mistake
recorded against `add_x16,x14,x16` in emit_advance_temp: emitted output that
"parsed and looked plausible for eighty walls and had never been assembled".

amd64. `mov %ebx,%ebx` is 89 /r with mod=11, reg=EBX, rm=EBX -- ModRM 0xDB --
so 89DB, next to the 89C0 that is already there for eax. Verified by
disassembly:

    89 c0    mov %eax,%eax
    89 db    mov %ebx,%ebx

aarch64. ORR Wd,WZR,Wm, the same encoding the existing `mov_w0,w0` uses:

    0x2A000000 | (Wm << 16) | (WZR << 5) | Wd

    mov_w0,w0    Wm=0 Wd=0    0x2A0003E0   e003002a   (already present)
    mov_w1,w1    Wm=1 Wd=1    0x2A0103E1   e103012a

There is no `uxtw` on either target and none is wanted: a 32-bit register
write zeroes the top half of the 64-bit register, so `mov w,w` and `mov e,e`
ARE the zero-extending moves. That is the same reasoning that gave the
unsigned four-byte entry in patch 0010 its odd name.

VERIFICATION IS UNEVEN AND SAYS SO. The amd64 entry is byte-anchored by
objdump above. The aarch64 entry is anchored by construction against the
mov_w0,w0 entry it mirrors, and then functionally: with it, uac-sweep.sh runs
12,960 points on aarch64 under qemu and agrees with gcc on all of them, which
it cannot do if the encoding is anything other than what its name says.
tcc-two-ways' "byte-anchored against real as" step is what will close the gap
properly, and it runs on a machine that has an aarch64 assembler.

